.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
Alias expansion in bash is a text substitution performed at parse time,
before any other expansions.
Aliases defined via this function affect subsequent \f[CR]bc_eval()\f[R]
calls in the same session.
.IP \(bu 2
Aliases are distinct from functions.
An alias and a function may share the same name; the alias takes
precedence during interactive command parsing.
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[B]value\f[R] parameter is stored literally.
The caller does not need to shell\-quote the value; quoting is only
needed in shell syntax (\f[CR]alias name=\(aqvalue\(aq\f[R]), not in the
C API.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no built\-in mechanism to make an alias readonly.
Unlike variables, aliases cannot have attributes.
.IP \(bu 2
The function uses the v2 binary protocol internally.
The STATE channel request includes \f[CR]\(dqop\(dq: \(dqset\(dq\f[R],
\f[CR]\(dqns\(dq: \(dqalias\(dq\f[R], \f[CR]\(dqname\(dq\f[R], and
\f[CR]\(dqvalue\(dq\f[R] fields.
.IP \(bu 2
A single \f[CR]bc_client_t\f[R] must not be used concurrently from
multiple threads.
